    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/biton-coulibaly"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/biton-coulibaly.jpg" alt="Bitòn Coulibaly" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/biton-coulibaly">Bitòn Coulibaly</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tombe_Biton_Coulibaly.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">KaTeznik, CC BY-SA 2.0 fr</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Bamana Empire, or Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segou">Ségou</a>, grew up among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people">Bamana</a> farmers on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> in the political vacuum left by the fall of the Songhay Empire to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in 1591. Its early history is known mainly from Bamana oral traditions and the epics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a>, written down from about 1900 by French colonial officials such as Charles Monteil and later by scholars; contemporary written evidence is thin, and the first detailed eyewitness description is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mungo-park">Mungo Park</a>’s, from 1796. According to the traditions, a mid-seventeenth-century leader, Kaladian Kulibali, first built a hegemony around Ségou that did not outlast him. Around 1712 his descendant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biton-coulibaly">Mamari Kulibali</a>, head of a <em>ton</em>, an association of young men of the same age, turned it into a permanent war band and made himself king, taking the title Biton. He enlarged his following with war captives and with men unable to pay their dues, who became the <em>tonjon</em>, soldiers bound to him personally. From his capital at Ségou-Koro, Biton (r. c. 1712–1755) overcame rival chiefs, built a fleet of war canoes with the Somono boatmen of the river and withstood an invasion from Kong, the Watara state to the south. Around 1754 he defeated the rival Massassi rulers of Kaarta to the north-west and captured their king, Foulakoro, leaving Ségou the dominant power between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamako">Bamako</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bambara-people.jpg" alt="Bambara people" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people">Bambara people</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BambaraSenegal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jeanniot (gravure), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segou">Ségou</a> joined the farming villages of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people">Bamana</a> countryside to a state that lived by war. Farmers grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> on the floodplains and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, which women spun late into the night, as Park observed; Fulbe herders kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, and Bozo and Somono fishermen and boatmen worked the river. The state’s campaigns brought in captives, who were drafted as soldiers, settled in farming villages that fed the court and the army, or sold. Muslim merchants known as Maraka, living in trading towns such as Sinsani (Sansanding) and Nyamina, bought captives and grain and dealt in Saharan salt, cloth, gold, horses and firearms, and many captives from the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> were sold on to the French and British posts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> and shipped across the Atlantic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cowrie-shell">Cowrie shells</a> served as money. Park described Ségou as four walled towns on both banks of the Niger with perhaps 30,000 inhabitants, mosques in every quarter and royal ferries whose fares of ten cowries a passenger brought the king a large revenue, and he wrote of “a prospect of civilisation and magnificence” he had not expected. Most Bamana followed their own religion, organised in initiation societies such as the <em>komo</em> and centred on power objects (<em>boliw</em>), while the Maraka and many traders were Muslims living under non-Muslim kings.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/djenne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/djenne.jpg" alt="Djenné" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moschee_von_Djenn%C3%A9.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">qiv, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segou">Ségou</a>’s power was the tonjon, a standing force of captive origin that owed its loyalty to the king and its living to war. Annual campaigns in the dry season struck neighbouring peoples and rebellious tributaries for captives and plunder, and Somono crews manned the war canoes that carried troops along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>. Towns were enclosed by high earthen walls, and wars often turned on the siege of such strongholds. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngolo-diarra">Ngolo Diarra</a> (r. 1766–c. 1790) the army reasserted Ségou’s authority over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a> and the Fulbe chiefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina">Massina</a>, and Ngolo died around 1790 returning from a campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a>. His son Monzon (r. c. 1790–1808) fought repeated wars with Kaarta: in 1796 Park found the roads between the two kingdoms closed because Monzon’s army had marched against Kaarta’s king, Desse Koro, whose fortress of Gedingooma it did not take, and around 1800 Monzon’s power reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>. The army’s limits showed in the nineteenth century. In 1818 the Fulbe cleric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-amadu">Seku Amadu</a> defeated the chiefs of Massina and their Ségou allies at Noukouma and founded the Massina Empire, with which Ségou fought for decades, and in 1860–1861 Umar Tall’s well-armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toucouleur">Toucouleur</a> army defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people">Bamana</a> at Woïtala and near Tio.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <em>faama</em>, or king, ruled with the backing of the tonjon and their commanders, who formed a military aristocracy able to make and unmake rulers. After Biton’s death in 1755 they killed his son Dinkoro within two years; his successor Ali, a Muslim who according to tradition tried to forbid the ancestral cults and the drinking of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a> beer, was also overthrown, and until 1766 the tonjon raised and removed a series of their own chiefs. Order returned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngolo-diarra">Ngolo Diarra</a>, who, the traditions say, had come to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segou">Ségou</a> as part of his village’s tribute and had risen among the tonjon. He seized power in 1766 and founded the Diarra dynasty, which ruled until 1861; by Park’s time the king resided at Ségou-Sikoro, one of the four towns of the capital. The heartland around Ségou was ruled directly, while conquered peoples kept their own chiefs and paid annual tribute, and Ngolo placed his sons in command of districts. No fixed rule settled the succession: Monzon had to defeat his brother Nianankoro before he could reign, and from the late 1830s kings followed one another in quick succession, with tonjon chiefs often deciding between rival princes. The kings also kept the royal <em>boliw</em>, the power objects at the centre of state ritual.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For much of the eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segou">Ségou</a> was the strongest state on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a>, and its wars reshaped the region’s population: captives taken by its armies filled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people">Bamana</a> villages and the ranks of the tonjon, or were sold into the Saharan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trades</a>, while Maraka towns along the river prospered as markets. As a powerful non-Muslim kingdom it became a target of Umar Tall, who presented his war as a holy war against pagans, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> spread widely among the Bamana only after the kingdom’s fall. The kingdom also gave the Bamana a political identity that outlived it. Bamanankan, the language of its kings and soldiers, is today spoken as a first or second language by about four Malians in five. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a>’ epics of Biton, Ngolo and Da Monzon form a celebrated cycle of West African oral literature; scholars recorded them in collections such as Lilyan Kesteloot’s <em>Da Monzon de Ségou</em> (1972) and David Conrad’s edition of the version told by the griot Tayiru Banbera (1990). Maryse Condé’s novel <em>Ségou</em> (1984) brought the kingdom to readers worldwide, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mungo-park">Mungo Park</a>’s <em>Travels in the Interior Districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a></em> (1799) gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> its first eyewitness picture of the middle Niger and its walled cities.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-archinard"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-archinard.jpg" alt="Louis Archinard" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-archinard">Louis Archinard</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_Archinard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown (Bain News Service, publisher), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segou">Ségou</a>’s decline began with the Fulbe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> in the inner <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> delta. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-amadu">Seku Amadu</a>’s victory at Noukouma in 1818 the Massina Empire, ruled from its new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdullahi">Hamdullahi</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djenne">Djenné</a> and the delta out of Ségou’s orbit, and the two states fought for decades. At home, rival princes and tonjon chiefs contested the throne. The decisive threat came from the west. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toucouleur">Toucouleur</a> cleric Umar Tall, who destroyed the Kingdom of Kaarta in 1854, turned on Ségou as a pagan state. In 1860 King Ali Diarra accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in return for help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massina">Massina</a>, the old enemy, but Umar’s army took Nyamina in May 1860, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people">Bamana</a> at Woïtala that September and crushed the combined Bamana and Massina forces near Tio in February 1861. Umar entered Ségou in March 1861 and made it the capital of the Toucouleur Empire, while Ali fled to Hamdullahi with the royal boliw; Massina itself fell to Umar in 1862. Bamana resistance continued in the countryside against Umar’s son Ahmadu and his governors until the French under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-archinard">Louis Archinard</a> took Ségou in April 1890. Ségou then passed under French colonial rule.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Bamana Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segou">Ségou</a>, built by Biton Kulibali and the captive soldiers of the tonjon, dominated the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> for much of the eighteenth century, fed the Saharan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trades</a>, held out against the Fulbe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihads</a> until Umar Tall’s conquest in 1861, and lives on in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people">Bamana</a> identity and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a>’ epics.</em></p>

